# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: Eight Mallets Before the Guitar Runs

**Story & Context:** The opening piece begins with eight measured strikes across the low jegog range. Joel is forbidden from entering until all eight notes have completed one full cycle. When the guitar arrives, it does not copy the bamboo rhythm; it threads a bright three-note sebene figure through the spaces immediately after each major strike.

Kadek adds muted baritone-guitar fifths beneath every second cycle, while Nia answers the fourth and eighth bamboo hits with group claps. Alain remains silent until the third full repetition, then enters with a soft bass-trombone pedal exactly when the deepest bamboo player drops out for one bar.

The central section increases guitar density every eight bars while the bamboo cycle remains unchanged. By the end, Joel is playing a rapid flowing line over the exact same eight-strike foundation heard at the beginning. The track closes by removing the guitar suddenly and leaving the bamboo cycle exposed at its original speed.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Eight chalk numbers marked above giant bamboo keys in an open repair yard, electric guitar neck crossing the frame, bass trombone bell reflecting red earth and afternoon light, handclap gesture blurred in motion. No full lyrics; sparse spoken cues limited to “eight first,” “after the wood,” “keep the cycle,” and “run now.”

## Track 2: Red Dust Under the Second String

**Story & Context:** During rehearsal, fine red dust from the yard gathers near the bridge of Kadek’s baritone guitar and slightly changes the attack of one string. He keeps the accident and builds a dry two-note figure around it. Made answers with a medium-register bamboo pattern that lands just before Kadek’s second note, creating a repeated push-pull cell.

Joel avoids full sebene speed at first, using sparse upper-register triad inversions. Nia introduces clap responses only after every fourth cycle. Alain adds short trombone falls that end before the deepest bamboo tone arrives, keeping the low register uncluttered.

Halfway through, Kadek wipes the dust away. The guitar attack becomes cleaner and brighter, which unexpectedly makes the groove feel less physical. After sixteen bars, he deliberately reapplies a small amount of dust to the bridge area and restores the rougher transient for the final section.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Macro documentary photograph of red earth dust near the bridge and second string of a baritone electric guitar, giant bamboo resonators behind it, padded mallets on a worktable, bass trombone slide catching warm light. Spoken fragments limited to “second string,” “leave the dust,” “clean once,” and “put the grain back.”

## Track 3: The Canopy Splits at Bar Thirty-Two

**Story & Context:** The EP closer is structured around a complete separation of the two rhythmic systems. For the first thirty-two bars, jegog and guitars play together: deep bamboo cycles, interlocking midrange strikes, clean soukous figures, restrained claps, and occasional bass-trombone pedals.

At bar thirty-two, the guitars stop completely. The jegog ensemble continues alone for sixteen bars, increasing internal interlock density without changing tempo. Then the bamboo drops out and the guitars take over the same duration, maintaining dance momentum without percussion by using palm-muted rhythm and rapid sebene loops.

The two sides return simultaneously but with their accents shifted relative to the opening. Alain holds one long low note through the recombination, functioning as the only continuous element between both halves. The EP ends with one dry bamboo strike followed by a single clean guitar harmonic.

